      "sequence_position": 15,
      "problem": "Let $a$ be a postive integer. Construct an expression that gives the last digit of $12a+11.$\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $b=12a+11.$ Note that $b$ is positive. Now, let ${d}_{3},$ ${d}_{2},$ and ${d}_{1}$ be the last three digits of the number $b:$ \n\n$$\nb=…{d}_{3}{d}_{2}{d}_{1}\n$$\n\nThen, $b$ can be written in expanded form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =⋯+100{d}_{3}+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} \\\\  & =10(⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the residue of $b$ modulo $10:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ≡10(⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0\\cdot (⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${d}_{1}$ is the last digit of $b.$\n\nFinally, since $b=12a+11,$ and \n\n$$\n12≡2,\\,11≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ 12a+11 & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ 2a+1 & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression that gives the last digit of $12a+11$ is the following:\n\n$$\n2a+1\\,\\text{mod}\\,10\n$$",
